. ❯ :help trim
trim, ltrim, rtrim  · Builtin operators and functions
  `trim` trims both leading and trailing whitespace.

  `ltrim` trims only leading (left side) whitespace.

  `rtrim` trims only trailing (right side) whitespace.

  Whitespace characters are the usual `" "`, `"\n"` `"\t"`, `"\r"`
  and also all characters in the Unicode character database with the
  whitespace property. Note that what considers whitespace might
  change in the future.

  [1] trim, ltrim, rtrim
      on " abc "  →  "abc", "abc ", " abc"
:try 1 runs an example on its own input; :try 1 . on yours
. ❯ :try 1
trim, ltrim, rtrim  on " abc "
"abc"
"abc "
" abc"
(as the manual says)
. ❯ :pp
"abc"
"abc "
" abc"
. ❯ :try 1 .
trim, ltrim, rtrim  on .
error: trim cannot be applied to: array ([{"people":[{"age":30,"na ...])
. ❯ .[0].team | "  \(.)  "
"  alpha  "
. ❯ :push
stage 1: .[0].team | "  \(.)  "  → string
.[0].team | "  \(.)  " ❯ :try 1 .
trim, ltrim, rtrim  on .
"alpha"
"alpha  "
"  alpha"
(3 outputs)
.[0].team | "  \(.)  " ❯ :pop
popped: .[0].team | "  \(.)  "
. ❯ :help first
first(expr), last(expr), nth(n; expr)  · Advanced features
  The `first(expr)` and `last(expr)` functions extract the first
  and last values from `expr`, respectively.

  The `nth(n; expr)` function extracts the nth value output by `expr`.
  Note that `nth(n; expr)` doesn't support negative values of `n`.

  [1] [first(range(.)), last(range(.)), nth(5; range(.))]
      on 10  →  [0,9,5]
  [2] [first(empty), last(empty), nth(5; empty)]
      on null  →  []

first, last, nth(n)  · Advanced features
  The `first` and `last` functions extract the first
  and last values from any array at `.`.

  The `nth(n)` function extracts the nth value of any array at `.`.

  [3] [range(.)]|[first, last, nth(5)]
      on 10  →  [0,9,5]
:try N runs an example on its own input; :try N . on yours
. ❯ :try 3
[range(.)]|[first, last, nth(5)]  on 10
[0,9,5]
(as the manual says)
. ❯ :try 9
usage: :try N or :try N . with N from 1 to 3
. ❯ :help keys
keys, keys_unsorted  · Builtin operators and functions
  The builtin function `keys`, when given an object, returns
  its keys in an array.

  The keys are sorted "alphabetically", by unicode codepoint
  order. This is not an order that makes particular sense in
  any particular language, but you can count on it being the
  same for any two objects with the same set of keys,
  regardless of locale settings.

  When `keys` is given an array, it returns the valid indices
  for that array: the integers from 0 to length-1.

  The `keys_unsorted` function is just like `keys`, but if
  the input is an object then the keys will not be sorted,
  instead the keys will roughly be in insertion order.
  ! keys_unsorted isn't in jqv: gojq doesn't keep key order in values; keys gives them sorted (Q-010)

  [1] keys
      on {"abc": 1, "abcd": 2, "Foo": 3}  →  ["Foo", "abc", "abcd"]
  [2] keys
      on [42,3,35]  →  [0,1,2]
:try N runs an example on its own input; :try N . on yours
. ❯ :help have_decnum
have_decnum  · Builtin operators and functions
  This builtin returns true if jq was built with "decnum",
  which is the current literal number preserving numeric
  backend implementation for jq.
  ! have_decnum isn't in jqv: gojq keeps big integers exact, and has no decimal-number mode to ask about
. ❯ :help splits
splits(regex), splits(regex; flags)  · Regular expressions
  These provide the same results as their `split` counterparts,
  but as a stream instead of an array.

  [1] splits(", *")
      on "ab,cd,   ef, gh"  →  "ab", "cd", "ef", "gh"
  [2] splits(",? *"; "n")
      on "ab,cd ef,  gh"  →  "ab", "cd", "ef", "gh"
      ! gojq differs: gojq has no "n" regex flag
:try N runs an example on its own input; :try N . on yours
. ❯ :try 2
splits(",? *"; "n")  on "ab,cd ef,  gh"
error: unsupported regular expression flag: "ng"
(the manual says "ab", "cd", "ef", "gh")
! gojq differs: gojq has no "n" regex flag
